<https://opus-project.eu/letter/Smith1_letter_104> a opus:LetterManifestation,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:abstract "In this letter to the Earl of Salisbury, dated June 22, 1607, Henry Wotton reports on the growing momentum of religious reform in Venice following its recent conflict with the Papacy. He notes that the Venetian state takes pride in its resolution of tensions with the Pope and anticipates a diplomatic response from King James I. Wotton expresses confidence that the Protestant cause is advancing in Venice, attributing this progress to the writings and private teachings of Maestro Paolo Sarpi and his associates. He draws a parallel between Sarpi’s efforts and the work of St. Paul in Corinth, emphasizing both the challenges and the potential for transformation in a city known for its moral laxity. Wotton closes by reaffirming his loyalty and service to Salisbury." ;
    bibo:edition "Pearsall Smith 1907, p. 490" ;
    skos:note "letter_17 in the old chart (Wotton_Jes_Letters)" ;
    opus:authorAsMarked "Henry Wotton" ;
    opus:dateAsMarked "22th of June, 1607." ;
    opus:dateIsUncertain true ;
    opus:destination [ opus:certainty "inferred" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/place/place_4> ] ;
    opus:destinationAsMarked "London" ;
    opus:incipit "This State will likewise shortly expect" ;
    opus:originAsMarked "Venice" ;
    opus:recipient [ opus:certainty "certain" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/person/person_199> ] ;
    opus:recipientAsMarked "Earl of Salisbury" ;
