<https://opus-project.eu/letter/Smith1_letter_109> a opus:LetterManifestation,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:abstract "In this letter of 13 September 1607, Henry Wotton, ambassador in Venice, writes to Robert Cecil, Earl of Salisbury, sending recently published books on the 1606–1607 papal–Venetian dispute and a private portrait of the Servite friar Paolo Sarpi. Wotton describes Sarpi as a secretly Protestant-leaning figure of deep learning, critical of papal authority, and well-regarded by the Venetian Senate. He urges Salisbury to secure a copy of the Pope’s breve against King James’s oath of allegiance—Sarpi intends to show it to the Senate as proof that Rome seeks to dissolve the sovereignty of princes. Wotton highlights the strategic importance of Sarpi’s covert collaboration with his own chaplain for “less observation” of their activities. He closes by commending a trusted messenger to Salisbury’s favor and requesting the breve be forwarded swiftly." ;
    bibo:edition "Pearsall Smith 1907, pp. 497-500" ;
    skos:note "letter_18 in the old chart (Wotton_Jes_Letters)" ;
    opus:authorAsMarked "Henry Wotton" ;
    opus:dateAsMarked "13th of September, 1607. Style of the place." ;
    opus:destination [ opus:certainty "inferred" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/place/place_4> ] ;
    opus:destinationAsMarked "London" ;
    opus:incipit "Your Lordship receiveth by this gentleman the books" ;
    opus:originAsMarked "Venice" ;
    opus:recipient [ opus:certainty "certain" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/person/person_199> ] ;
    opus:recipientAsMarked "Earl of Salisbury" ;
    schema:author [ opus:certainty "certain" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/person/person_110> ] ;
