<https://opus-project.eu/letter/Smith1_letter_113> a opus:LetterManifestation,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:abstract "Sir Henry Wotton, the English ambassador to Venice, updates Robert Cecil (the Earl of Salisbury) on the aftermath of the attempted assassination of Fra Paolo Sarpi. In Letter 113 (1 November 1607), Wotton reports Sarpi’s near recovery and notes that both the Council of Ten and the Venetian Senate are publicly reaffirming their protection of him. Wotton names three fugitive suspects—“Poma, the priest, and the Florentine”—last sighted traveling through Ancona and arriving in Rome armed with prohibited weapons, implying papal collusion. The Venetian ambassador in Rome has been instructed to spare no expense to uncover who orchestrated the attack." ;
    bibo:edition "Pearsall Smith 1907, pp. 510-511" ;
    skos:note "letter_21 in the old chart (Wotton_Jes_Letters)" ;
    opus:dateAsMarked "the first of November, 1607. Style of the place." ;
    opus:destination [ opus:certainty "inferred" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/place/place_4> ] ;
    opus:destinationAsMarked "London" ;
    opus:incipit "For the course of things in this place" ;
    opus:originAsMarked "Venice" ;
    opus:recipient [ opus:certainty "certain" ;
