<https://opus-project.eu/letter/Smith1_letter_118> a opus:LetterManifestation,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:abstract "This letter provides a snapshot of Venetian politics in early 1607, showcasing Wotton’s continued engagement in espionage and diplomatic maneuvering. The harsh sentencing of Angelo Badoero highlights Venice’s strict control over internal affairs, while Wotton’s depiction of Archduke Ferdinand as a Jesuit puppet aligns with broader English fears of Catholic influence. The dramatic account of Sir Julius Caesar’s son’s death adds an element of personal tragedy, linking the diplomatic narrative to English interests. Wotton’s conspiratorial mindset remains evident in his framing of events. He portrays the Jesuits as omnipresent manipulators, the Papal Nuncio as a scheming but ineffective agent, and Venice as heroically resisting foreign encroachment. His letter serves both as intelligence and as a piece of rhetorical persuasion, reinforcing England’s perception of Catholic powers as inherently conspiratorial." ;
    bibo:edition "Pearsall Smith 1907, pp. 514-516" ;
    skos:note "letter_23 in the old chart (Wotton_Jes_Letters)" ;
    opus:authorAsMarked "Henry Wotton" ;
    opus:dateAsMarked "18th of January, 1607⟨8⟩. Style of the place." ;
    opus:dateIsInferred true ;
    opus:destination [ opus:certainty "inferred" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/place/place_4> ] ;
    opus:destinationAsMarked "London" ;
    opus:incipit "The last week we had the letters of Flanders very late" ;
    opus:originAsMarked "Venice" ;
    opus:recipient [ opus:certainty "certain" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/person/person_199> ] ;
    opus:recipientAsMarked "Earl of Salisbury" ;
