<https://opus-project.eu/letter/Smith1_letter_144> a opus:LetterManifestation,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:abstract "In this dispatch of fifth December 1608 from Venice, Sir Henry Wotton informs the Earl of Salisbury that Ridolfo Poma, implicated in the failed attempt on Fra Paolo Sarpi’s life, now enjoys guarded favor in Rome, causing the Pope an awkward dilemma between leniency and exposing the Church’s role in sponsoring assassins. Wotton further reveals two new conspiracies uncovered against Sarpi, including one hatched by his own “converse” and relatives from Viterbo. Meanwhile, Lord Roos has arrived in Venice and fallen ill, yet demonstrates a resolute aversion to Catholicism. Finally, Wotton outlines a covert plan to free the imprisoned Mr. Mole by funneling funds through Nicholas Fitzherbert, who hopes to regain King James’s favor." ;
    bibo:edition "Pearsall Smith 1907, pp. 559-560" ;
    skos:note "letter_27 in the old chart (Wotton_Jes_Letters)" ;
    opus:dateAsMarked "5th of December, 1608." ;
    opus:dateIsUncertain true ;
    opus:destination [ opus:certainty "inferred" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/place/place_4> ] ;
    opus:destinationAsMarked "London" ;
    opus:incipit "From Rome we hear that Poma" ;
    opus:originAsMarked "Venice" ;
    opus:recipient [ opus:certainty "certain" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/person/person_199> ] ;
    opus:recipientAsMarked "Earl of Salisbury" ;
    schema:author [ opus:certainty "inferred" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/person/person_110> ] ;
    schema:dateCreated "1608-12-05"^^xsd:date ;
    schema:inLanguage "English" ;
    schema:locationCreated [ opus:certainty "certain" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/place/place_1> ] .

<https://opus-project.eu/letter/Smith1_letter_145> a opus:LetterManifestation,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:abstract "In this letter, Wotton informs the Earl of Salisbury about a significant defection in Venice: Monsignor Ribetta, the vicar of the Patriarch, has fled to Rome under the influence of the Patriarch himself, who is allegedly seeking a cardinalship. The Senate reacts to this betrayal by curbing the Patriarch’s financial privileges rather than taking harsher measures. Wotton draws a parallel to Henry VIII’s dissolution of monasteries, suggesting that this restriction will prevent the return of the Jesuits. He also expresses gratitude for Salisbury’s support of his countryman, John Finett, and concludes with a reference to his own unprofitability in service." ;
    bibo:edition "Pearsall Smith 1907, pp. 560-563" ;
    skos:note "letter_26 in the old chart (Wotton_Jes_Letters)" ;
    opus:authorAsMarked "Henry Wotton" ;
    opus:dateAsMarked "⟨Dec., 1608.⟩" ;
    opus:dateIsApproximate true ;
    opus:destination [ opus:certainty "inferred" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/place/place_4> ] ;
    opus:destinationAsMarked "London" ;
    opus:incipit "Having both extraordinary occasion and a fit opportunity" ;
    opus:originAsMarked "Venice" ;
    opus:recipient [ opus:certainty "certain" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/person/person_199> ] ;
    opus:recipientAsMarked "Earl of Salisbury" ;
    schema:author [ opus:certainty "certain" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/person/person_110> ] ;
    schema:dateCreated "1608-12"^^xsd:gYear ;
    schema:inLanguage "English" ;
    schema:locationCreated [ opus:certainty "inferred" ;
