<https://opus-project.eu/letter/Smith1_letter_148> a opus:LetterManifestation,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:abstract "Henry Wotton introduces Giovanni Francesco Biondi to King James I, describing him as a learned and devout man from Dalmatia who has sought refuge in England to practice his religion more freely. Biondi had previously served as secretary to Cavaliere Pietro Priuli, the Venetian ambassador to France, and had maintained contact with English agents, notably Sir George Carew. Biondi had risked his life to transport a Protestant nobleman’s library from France to Venice and had maintained ties with Reformed Churches in the Grisons, Geneva, and France. Based on these qualities, Wotton recommends him to James I. Additionally, Maestro Paulo (Paolo Sarpi), a key Venetian reformer and anti-papal thinker, entrusts Biondi to deliver certain proposals to the English monarch for broader religious and political purposes. Wotton underscores Biondi’s integrity and loyalty to Protestant causes, asking the King to receive him favorably." ;
    bibo:edition "Pearsall Smith 1907, pp. 566-567" ;
    skos:note "letter_43 in the old chart (Wotton_Jes_Letters)" ;
    opus:dateAsMarked "16th of January, 1608⟨9⟩. Style of the place." ;
    opus:dateIsInferred true ;
    opus:destination [ opus:certainty "inferred" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/place/place_4> ] ;
    opus:destinationAsMarked "London" ;
    opus:incipit "The presenter hereof (by name Francesco Biondi)" ;
    opus:originAsMarked "Venice" ;
    opus:recipient [ opus:certainty "certain" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/person/person_119> ] ;
    opus:recipientAsMarked "James I" ;
    schema:author [ opus:certainty "inferred" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/person/person_110> ] ;
    schema:dateCreated "1609-01-16"^^xsd:date ;
    schema:inLanguage "English" ;
    schema:locationCreated [ opus:certainty "certain" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/place/place_1> ] .

<https://opus-project.eu/letter/Smith1_letter_149> a opus:LetterManifestation,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:abstract "Henry Wotton reports to James I about the preaching of Fra Fulgenzio Micanzio, a Venetian Servite friar, who delivers sermons at St. Lorenzo promoting Protestant-leaning theology. His preaching, which emphasizes scriptural authority over papal doctrine, has drawn large audiences, including many Venetian senators and gentlemen. The Inquisition attempted to silence Micanzio, but the Duke of Venice protected him. The Venetian Senate, in response to the papal opposition, doubled the salaries of their state-sponsored theologians and reaffirmed their commitment to religious independence from Rome. Wotton also recounts a humorous episode involving a Venetian noblewoman, a Mocenigo, who bequeathed 10,000 crowns to the Jesuits, but with the condition that they could only receive it upon their return to Venice. Given the Venetian state’s expulsion of the Jesuits, this effectively ensured they would never reclaim the money, inadvertently strengthening Venice’s stance against the Jesuits." ;
    bibo:edition "Pearsall Smith 1907, pp. 567-570" ;
    skos:note "letter_44 in the old chart (Wotton_Jes_Letters)" ;
    opus:authorAsMarked "Ottavio Baldi" ;
    opus:dateAsMarked "20th of March, 1609." ;
    opus:dateIsUncertain true ;
    opus:destination [ opus:certainty "inferred" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/place/place_4> ] ;
    opus:destinationAsMarked "London" ;
    opus:incipit "Being most assured that I shall yield your Majesty" ;
    opus:originAsMarked "Venice" ;
    opus:recipient [ opus:certainty "certain" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/person/person_119> ] ;
    opus:recipientAsMarked "James I" ;
    schema:author [ opus:certainty "certain" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/person/person_110> ] ;
    schema:dateCreated "1609-03-20"^^xsd:date ;
    schema:inLanguage "English" ;
    schema:locationCreated [ opus:certainty "certain" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/place/place_1> ] .

