<https://opus-project.eu/letter/Smith1_letter_153> a opus:LetterManifestation,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:abstract "In this letter from Venice, Henry Wotton informs the Earl of Salisbury about recent events concerning the punishment of friars. A friar who had been exiled for aiding secret communications between the Papal Nuncio and Angelo Badoero was caught returning and sentenced to ten years of imprisonment. Another monk from Perugia, allegedly involved in a plot to assassinate Maestro Paulo (Paolo Sarpi), was executed in secrecy by the Venetian Council of Ten. Wotton notes that while these occurrences may seem minor compared to the affairs of other English agents abroad, he believes they signal larger developments in Venice. He then requests a brief hiatus in his correspondence, citing medical advice to visit the Baths of Abano for treatment of a suspected kidney or bladder ailment." ;
    bibo:edition "Pearsall Smith 1907, pp. 576-577" ;
    skos:note "letter_29 in the old chart (Wotton_Jes_Letters)" ;
    opus:authorAsMarked "Henry Wotton" ;
    opus:dateAsMarked "1st of May, 1609." ;
    opus:dateIsUncertain true ;
    opus:destination [ opus:certainty "inferred" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/place/place_4> ] ;
    opus:destinationAsMarked "London" ;
    opus:incipit "They have here within these two days" ;
    opus:originAsMarked "Venice" ;
