<https://opus-project.eu/letter/Smith1_letter_155> a opus:LetterManifestation,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:abstract "Henry Wotton relays Maestro Paolo (Paolo Sarpi)’s suggestion that England mediate between the Dutch Republic and Venice by encouraging the former to report on their treaty negotiations, subtly fostering closer ties without Venice appearing to seek them directly. Sarpi argues that England, as the “balancer of Christendom,” is best positioned for such a role, allowing Venice to maintain a posture of passive prudence while still benefiting from the alliance. Wotton questions why Venice does not initiate this exchange through its own ambassador in England, but Sarpi insists that Venetian diplomacy prefers indirect engagement. The proposal extends to Brandenburg’s claim to Cleves, recognizing shifting German alliances as another diplomatic opportunity. Wotton closes by noting that Giovanni Francesco Biondi, if still in England, could prepare the ground for these negotiations as part of his upcoming travels. The letter reflects the covert nature of early modern diplomacy, where states maneuvered carefully to advance their interests while maintaining strategic discretion." ;
    bibo:edition "Pearsall Smith 1907, pp. 578-580" ;
    skos:note "letter_30 in the old chart (Wotton_Jes_Letters)" ;
    opus:dateAsMarked "⟨June? 1609.⟩" ;
    opus:dateIsApproximate true ;
    opus:destination [ opus:certainty "inferred" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/place/place_4> ] ;
    opus:destinationAsMarked "London" ;
    opus:incipit "Postscript. — Before the departure of this bearer" ;
    opus:originAsMarked "Venice" ;
    opus:recipient [ opus:certainty "certain" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/person/person_199> ] ;
    opus:recipientAsMarked "Earl of Salisbury" ;
