<https://opus-project.eu/letter/Smith1_letter_156> a opus:LetterManifestation,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:abstract "In this letter, Wotton expresses deep concern over the systematic targeting of English Protestant travelers in Catholic Europe, particularly their tutors, whom he claims are being deliberately separated from their students by Jesuit-influenced authorities. He recounts the arrest of Edward Lichefeld by Dominican friars and his imprisonment by the Inquisition, paralleling it with similar cases, including that of Mr. Mole in Rome. Wotton portrays the Jesuits as covert manipulators within the Papal court, orchestrating intelligence operations to preemptively monitor and influence English noblemen before they arrive in Rome. His language reflects both real geopolitical tensions and conspiratorial anxieties, particularly regarding the supposed “reconciliation” of Lichefeld within the Jesuit novitiate, which he suspects to be a strategic fabrication. He highlights the broader trend of religious surveillance and coercion, linking these events to a calculated strategy aimed at undermining Protestant influence in the region. Throughout, Wotton grapples with uncertainty, admitting his growing difficulty in distinguishing reality from deception, reinforcing his broader narrative of Catholic intrigue." ;
    bibo:edition "Pearsall Smith 1907, pp. 580-582" ;
    skos:note "letter_31 in the old chart (Wotton_Jes_Letters)" ;
    opus:dateAsMarked "⟨June 18, 1609, N.S.⟩" ;
