<https://opus-project.eu/letter/Smith1_letter_164> a opus:LetterManifestation,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:abstract "In this letter, Henry Wotton describes his formal presentation of King James I’s book to the Doge of Venice, framing the occasion as an auspicious event occurring on St. James’s Day. Wotton elaborates on the significance of monarchs engaging in writing, reinforcing James’s literary work as a testament to his wisdom and leadership. He draws a direct connection between the book and England’s stance on sovereignty, particularly in response to the Papal influence, likening the Pope’s interference in secular affairs to the uncontrollable flooding of the Tiber. Wotton highlights his own role in ensuring that the Doge correctly understands James’s intent—not to interfere with Venetian governance but to assert the necessity of resistance against Papal overreach. The letter reflects Wotton’s diplomatic tact, emphasizing the importance of Venice’s recognition of James’s support while securing Venetian goodwill in the broader Protestant-Catholic struggle." ;
    bibo:edition "Pearsall Smith 1907, pp. 590-593" ;
    skos:note "letter_32 in the old chart (Wotton_Jes_Letters)" ;
    opus:dateAsMarked "Last of July, 1609. Style of the place." ;
    opus:destination [ opus:certainty "inferred" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/place/place_4> ] ;
    opus:destinationAsMarked "London" ;
    opus:incipit "This worthy Duke," ;
    opus:originAsMarked "Venice" ;
    opus:recipient [ opus:certainty "certain" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/person/person_199> ] ;
    opus:recipientAsMarked "Earl of Salisbury" ;
    schema:author [ opus:certainty "inferred" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/person/person_110> ] ;
    schema:dateCreated "1609-07-31"^^xsd:date ;
    schema:inLanguage "English" ;
    schema:locationCreated [ opus:certainty "inferred" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/place/place_1> ] .

<https://opus-project.eu/letter/Smith1_letter_165> a opus:LetterManifestation,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:abstract "In this letter of 14 August 1609, Henry Wotton (signing himself as “Ottavio Baldi”) writes to King James I from Venice. He reports that the Pope has issued a “censure” against the King’s recently published book—one that defends the English Oath of Allegiance and clarifies James’s religious stance. The Pope personally gave a copy of this condemnation to the Venetian ambassador’s secretary, intending to prevent Venice from receiving James’s “blessed labours.” Wotton relays that covert demand for King James’s book is high, and many in Venice secretly desire copies. Wotton’s chaplain has translated the King’s work into Italian, seeking input from Fra Paolo Sarpi and Fulgenzio Micanzio. Both friars admire James’s confessional summary and see him as especially suited, “above all [his] predecessors,” to extend true religion not just in Europe but also in America (“Nova Britannia”). Meanwhile, Wotton thanks the King for letting him remain longer in Venice before returning to England. The postscript adds that the Grand Duke of Tuscany has burned James’s book under the Inquisition’s direction, the Spanish ambassador claims the Duke of Savoy rejected it, and in Rome it was publicly banned and equated with Beza’s Confessions. Yet Wotton suggests that such prohibitions only heighten curiosity among curious Venetians." ;
    bibo:edition "Pearsall Smith 1907, pp. 593-595" ;
    skos:note "letter_45 in the old chart (Wotton_Jes_Letters)" ;
    opus:authorAsMarked "Ottavio Baldi" ;
    opus:dateAsMarked "14th of August, 1609. Style of the place." ;
    opus:destination [ opus:certainty "inferred" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/place/place_4> ] ;
    opus:destinationAsMarked "London" ;
    opus:incipit "I have now by secret means gotten a copy of that censure" ;
    opus:originAsMarked "Venice" ;
    opus:recipient [ opus:certainty "certain" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/person/person_119> ] ;
    opus:recipientAsMarked "James I" ;
    schema:author [ opus:certainty "certain" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/person/person_110> ] ;
