<https://opus-project.eu/letter/Smith1_letter_167> a opus:LetterManifestation,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:abstract "In this letter, Henry Wotton informs the Earl of Salisbury about the prohibition of King James I’s book, Apologia pro iureamento fidelitatis, in Venice. He attributes the censorship to the Papal Nuncio, who allegedly took advantage of the Doge’s illness to secure the Venetian College’s approval for the ban. Wotton expresses both skepticism and frustration, noting that while the prohibition was issued, it did not explicitly name James I as the author, nor did it impose penalties on booksellers. He employs an informant, Francesco Biondi, to investigate, revealing that copies are expected from Basel, and that a prominent bookseller, Giambattista Ciotti, remains willing to supply them. Wotton characterizes Ciotti as “Jesuitical,” reinforcing his anti-Jesuit conspiratorial lens. He resolves to publicly protest the prohibition and promises a detailed report to James I in his next letter. The letter blends genuine diplomatic concerns with Wotton’s conspiratorial interpretation of Catholic influence in Venice." ;
    bibo:edition "Pearsall Smith 1907, pp. 597-599" ;
    skos:note "letter_33 in the old chart (Wotton_Jes_Letters)" ;
    opus:authorAsMarked "Henry Wotton" ;
    opus:dateAsMarked "28th of August, 1609." ;
    opus:dateIsUncertain true ;
    opus:destination [ opus:certainty "inferred" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/place/place_4> ] ;
    opus:destinationAsMarked "London" ;
    opus:incipit "It may please his Majesty to be informed that," ;
    opus:originAsMarked "Venice" ;
    opus:recipient [ opus:certainty "certain" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/person/person_199> ] ;
    opus:recipientAsMarked "Earl of Salisbury" ;
    schema:author [ opus:certainty "certain" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/person/person_110> ] ;
    schema:dateCreated "1609-08-28"^^xsd:date ;
