<https://opus-project.eu/letter/Smith1_letter_66> a opus:LetterManifestation,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:abstract "In this letter, Henry Wotton writes to the Earl of Salisbury, enclosing two intercepted papers, one of which is an original letter sent from Rome to the Jesuit Possevino. Wotton admits to having developed a keen interest in acquiring such intercepted communications, particularly those involving the Jesuits. While he does not fully identify the sender of the letter, he notes that only the first two letters of the name are visible—if they are indeed initials and not coded characters. His remarks suggest an ongoing surveillance operation targeting Catholic networks, reflecting the broader anti-Jesuit and anti-papal sentiments of English intelligence efforts during the early 17th century. The letter exemplifies Wotton’s involvement in espionage and his focus on uncovering Jesuit activities, which were viewed as subversive by the English Crown." ;
    dcterms:subject <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/conspiracy>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/espionage>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/intercepted_correspondence> ;
    bibo:edition "Pearsall Smith 1907, pp. 422-424" ;
