<https://opus-project.eu/letter/Smith1_letter_68> a opus:LetterManifestation,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:abstract "Wotton reports to the Earl of Salisbury on the final expulsion of the Jesuits from Venice following their refusal to perform ecclesiastical duties after the enforcement of the Pope’s interdict. The Venetian Senate decreed their removal, allowing them to take only their religious garments and breviaries while an official inventory of their possessions was taken. Prior to leaving, a leading Jesuit, Il Padre Barone, sought an audience with the Prince, offering Jesuit mediation between Venice and the Pope. The Prince firmly rejected the offer, asserting that Venice had never relied on such intermediaries and would not start now. Wotton describes his own role in justifying English policies against the Jesuits, refuting accusations against King James I. He highlights his speech before the Venetian Senate, where he defended England’s anti-Jesuit statutes and portrayed the Jesuits as deceptive figures manipulating public opinion. His address was well received, and the Venetian Prince reassured him of the Republic’s high regard for James I, recognizing his leadership as divinely protected. Wotton frames the Jesuits’ expulsion as a triumph against Catholic influence, reinforcing the anti-Jesuit sentiment common in early seventeenth-century Protestant diplomacy." ;
    dcterms:subject <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/diplomacy>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/international_politics>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/politics>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/religion>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/venetian_interdict> ;
