<https://opus-project.eu/letter/Smith1_letter_7> a opus:LetterManifestation,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:abstract "Henry Wotton writes to Lord Zouche from Ingolstadt on October 27, 1590, providing an update on his diplomatic observations. He describes his reception upon delivering Lord Zouche’s letters, noting his interactions with Hubert van Giffen, a prominent jurist at Ingolstadt. Wotton then recounts his encounter with a disgraced Englishman—likely Anthony Sherley—who resents Queen Elizabeth I, blaming her for confiscating his family’s estates and spreading anti-Elizabethan rhetoric. Wotton implies that this Englishman has received “dangerous advice” from his father and suggests that his presence abroad is politically motivated rather than merely personal. The letter also contains intelligence about the papal election, reporting the false news that Cardinal di Santa Severina had been elected Pope under the name Julius IV. He notes the Jesuit College’s reaction to the election, implying Jesuit influence in the process. Wotton promises to send more details from Vienna and closes by reaffirming his loyalty to Lord Zouche." ;
    dcterms:subject <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/diplomacy>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/international_politics>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/papacy>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/papal_conclave>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/politics> ;
