<https://opus-project.eu/letter/Smith1_letter_72> a opus:LetterManifestation,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:abstract "Henry Wotton writes to the Earl of Salisbury from Venice, reporting his recent activities there, including several audiences in the Pien Collegio (Full College). He mentions the current situation concerning English harbours, which have since the 18th of August 1604 been open to ships from other countries, allowing foreign Princes (including the Pope), to exert their influence on the realms of King James I. Wotton announces the upcoming arrival of certain English merchants, who are meant to deliver an intercepted letter from Rome, which is too important to be sent by ordinary means. Lastly he mentions another dispatch of his (likely a letter to James I dated June 16), written in cipher and detailing other matters which are still ongoing. " ;
    dcterms:subject <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/cipher>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/espionage>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/intercepted_correspondence>,
