<https://opus-project.eu/letter/Smith1_letter_73> a opus:LetterManifestation,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:abstract "Henry Wotton writes from Venice, reporting the deepening conflict between the Venetian Republic and the Papacy, which he believes will soon escalate into open warfare after the harvest season. The Venetian Senate has officially expelled the Jesuits from all its territories and enacted stringent legal barriers to prevent their return. The Doge, Leonardo Donato, is lauded as a resolute and highly capable leader, likened to a “Dictator of the State,” steering Venice through this crisis with political acumen and a supposed enlightenment in religious truth. In response, Rome has begun considering a formal ecclesiastical trial against Donato, though its outcome remains uncertain. Wotton hints at the wider implications of these tensions, particularly in how they may affect broader political and religious dynamics in Italy and beyond." ;
    dcterms:subject <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/international_politics>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/politics>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/venetian_interdict>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/war> ;
    bibo:edition "Pearsall Smith 1907, pp. 434-436" ;
    skos:note "letter_10 in the old chart (Wotton_Jes_Letters)" ;
