<https://opus-project.eu/letter/Smith1_letter_74> a opus:LetterManifestation,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:abstract "Henry Wotton reports to the Earl of Salisbury on the continuing conflict between Venice and the Jesuits, focusing on the case of Padre Gagliardo, a Jesuit exiled from Venice and now preaching against the Republic in Mantua. The Duke of Mantua, himself a Venetian noble, has expelled these Jesuits from his territories in defense of Venice’s honor. In response, the Venetian Senate has issued a rare public proclamation summoning Gagliardo to appear within six days or face proscription and an extraordinary bounty on his head. This decision, Wotton notes, is significant because it defies papal authority, directly opposing one of the original justifications for the Pope’s excommunication of Venice. Wotton also reports that Venice has formally warned the French ambassador, De Fresne-Canaye, to cease interceding on behalf of the Pope. The letter suggests that tensions may soon escalate into military conflict, though the likelihood of peace in Hungary and the withdrawal of Dutch forces from Spain may temper hostilities. Wotton encloses contemporary satirical epigrams and political commentary with his letter, promising further updates when events become more concrete." ;
    dcterms:subject <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/crime>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/international_politics>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/politics>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/religion>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/venetian_interdict>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/war> ;
