<https://opus-project.eu/letter/Smith1_letter_76> a opus:LetterManifestation,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:abstract "In this letter, Henry Wotton writes to the Earl of Salisbury, informing him of the tidings received recently in Venice, that the Spanish fleet has sailed to Greece and sacked Durazzo, which will likely lead to Venice being attacked in retaliation by the Ottoman Empire. Wotton also expresses his great excitement, caused not only by the turbulent state of affairs in Italy, but also by the letter he recently received from king James I, in which the monarch has conveyed his approval towards Wotton, filling him with joy and pride. Finally, Wotton promises to give in another letter an account of the fullfilment of the instructions sent from London. " ;
    dcterms:subject <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/cipher>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/international_politics>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/politics>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/war> ;
