<https://opus-project.eu/letter/Smith1_letter_77> a opus:LetterManifestation,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:abstract "Henry Wotton writes to the Earl of Salisbury from Venice on August 25, 1606, enclosing two intercepted letters—one referencing Salisbury and the other Wotton himself. He expresses pride in being a target of the Jesuits’ suspicion and aligns his intelligence efforts with Salisbury’s. Wotton details his extensive spy network across key European cities (Rome, Milan, Turin, and Frankfurt), emphasizing his knowledge of Jesuit correspondence, seals, and confidants. Given the increasing costs of espionage, he requests additional secret-service funds from the Crown, citing James I’s approval of such intelligence gathering. The letter highlights the importance of espionage and interception in countering perceived Catholic threats, particularly the Jesuits, amid ongoing political and religious tensions between Venice, England, and Rome." ;
    dcterms:subject <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/cipher>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/espionage>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/financial_problems>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/intercepted_correspondence> ;
    bibo:edition "Pearsall Smith 1907, pp. 441-442" ;
    skos:note "letter_12 in the old chart (Wotton_Jes_Letters)" ;
