<https://opus-project.eu/letter/Smith1_letter_80> a opus:LetterManifestation,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:abstract "Henry Wotton writes to the Earl of Salisbury from Venice on September 22, 1606, in response to his earlier dispatch from July 31. He reports on an audience in the Venetian Pien Collegio (Full College), where he represented king James I, offering support for Venice in the ongoing conflict with Pope Paul V. Wotton excuses himself for making assurances on behalf of the monarch, maintaining he only did so in service of his interests. He describes the positive response from the Doge and the College, who have also received favourable news from their own ambassador in England, Zorzi Giustinian (who has been graciously received by James I), and have in turn offered their support to the English king. Wotton concludes the letter by introducing its bearer and Wotton's own chaplain, Nathaniel Fletcher, son of Richard Fletcher, Bishop of London. Fletcher is presented as the first preacher of the Church of England beyond the Alps, who accompanied Wotton for the last two years and has personally witnessed the recent confict between Venice and the Pope, which Wotton hopes will continue idefinitely. Wotton praises Fletcher's counsel during their stay in Italy, recommending him to the list of the preachers at the English Court for the next Lent as a person wholly worthy of that honour. " ;
    dcterms:subject <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/anglicanism>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/catholicism>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/diplomacy>,
