<https://opus-project.eu/letter/Smith1_letter_81> a opus:LetterManifestation,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:abstract "Henry Wotton writes to the Earl of Salisbury from Venice on September 22, 1606, telling him of a curious event which has transpired recently in Venice. An astrologer by the name of Benedetto Altavilla visited a member of the Senate, warning him about a plan to blow up the members of Venetian nobility who were to be present the next day in the hall of the Gran Concilio. The senator has indeed found a store of gunpowder in the designated place, but the Council of Ten, instead of rewarding the astrologer, deemed him the likely culprit. However, the Vincentine has turned out to be quite resistant to torture, which some attribute to his magical powers. Currently he has been set free and continues to affirm his prophetic powers. Wotton suspects that he indeed was the one who staged the whole plot he supposedly foresaw, probably in hopes of a reward and likely taking inspiration from the recent Gunpowder Plot. " ;
    dcterms:subject <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/assassination_plot>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/astrology>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/crime>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/gunpowder_plot>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/torture> ;
