<https://opus-project.eu/letter/Smith1_letter_82> a opus:LetterManifestation,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:abstract "Henry Wotton informs the Earl of Salisbury about his decision to send Rowland Woodward back to England, carrying important intelligence, including a Latin document containing the Spanish Ambassador’s opinion on the execution of Henry Garnet. Wotton expresses deep skepticism about the reliability of Jesuit narratives, after comparing the Spanish account with the testimonies of Captain Nicolas Pinner and his servant, both eyewitnesses to the execution. He finds the Spanish report, particularly concerning the alleged silence of the crowd, to be deliberately false. Wotton cynically states that he has learned from Jesuits to believe nothing from “the contrary side.” He also emphasizes that his intelligence reports hold value primarily because they pass through Salisbury’s scrutiny. Finally, Wotton attaches a letter to James I, leaving it unsealed for Salisbury’s review." ;
    dcterms:subject <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/cipher>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/conspiracy>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/disinformation>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/espionage>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/execution>,
        <https://opus-project.eu/keyword/intercepted_correspondence> ;
    bibo:edition "Pearsall Smith 1907, pp. 449-451" ;
    skos:note "letter_13 in the old chart (Wotton_Jes_Letters)" ;
