<https://opus-project.eu/letter/Smith1_letter_83> a opus:LetterManifestation,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:abstract "Henry Wotton, writing under his alias Ottavio Baldi, informs King James I that he has obtained, through secret channels, a discourse titled I Disegni di Spagna (The Designs of Spain), allegedly written by the Doge of Venice. Wotton provides both the original and his translation, inviting James I to assess whether the argument’s strength lies in its reasoning or its rhetoric. Wotton emphasizes the political implications of the text, particularly its bearing on the balance of power in Christendom. Wotton expresses his belief that the Jesuits were the main instigators of the ongoing conflict between Venice and the Papacy, not merely as agents of the Pope or Spain, but out of their own ambition. He suggests that the Jesuits’ role was either to divert the Pope from ruling in favor of the Dominicans in their doctrinal dispute or to secure control over papal decision-making through their extensive international intelligence network. He closes by humbly seeking pardon for his candid observations, attributing them to his unwavering loyalty to James I." ;
    bibo:edition "Pearsall Smith 1907, pp. 452-453" ;
    skos:note "letter_41 in the old chart (Wotton_Jes_Letters)" ;
    opus:authorAsMarked "Ottavio Baldi" ;
    opus:dateAsMarked "3 of November, 1606." ;
    opus:dateIsUncertain true ;
    opus:destination [ opus:certainty "inferred" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/place/place_4> ] ;
    opus:destinationAsMarked "London" ;
    opus:incipit "Having here by secret means gotten the copy of a discourse" ;
    opus:originAsMarked "Venice" ;
    opus:recipient [ opus:certainty "certain" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/person/person_119> ] ;
    opus:recipientAsMarked "James I" ;
