<https://opus-project.eu/letter/Smith1_letter_85> a opus:LetterManifestation,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:abstract "In this letter to the Earl of Salisbury, Henry Wotton reports the arrest of Captain William Turner, an English spy who had been engaged in covert activities on behalf of the Jesuits and the Archduke Ferdinand of Styria. Turner had infiltrated Venice under the pretense of religious persecution and attempted to pass intelligence regarding English and Venetian military movements to the Archduke. His correspondence with an English Jesuit in Gratz and his attempt to destroy sensitive documents upon capture confirmed his clandestine role. Wotton, having intercepted Turner’s communications and placed him in prison, expresses his belief that the Jesuits were manipulating foreign courts for their own advantage. The letter reveals Venice’s intensified surveillance of suspected Papal and Spanish agents, reflecting the broader tensions between Venice, the Papacy, and Spain. Wotton also hints at the possibility of flipping Turner into a double agent, acknowledging that informants are often as necessary as honest men in intelligence work." ;
    bibo:edition "Pearsall Smith 1907, pp. 453-456" ;
    skos:note "letter_14 in the old chart (Wotton_Jes_Letters)" ;
    opus:dateAsMarked "17 of November, 1606. Style of the place." ;
    opus:destination [ opus:certainty "inferred" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/place/place_4> ] ;
    opus:destinationAsMarked "London" ;
