<https://opus-project.eu/letter/Smith1_letter_89> a opus:LetterManifestation,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:abstract "In this letter to the Earl of Salisbury, Henry Wotton details his diplomatic stance amid the Venetian quarrel with the Pope, balancing his role between advocating for Venice’s autonomy and maintaining England’s interests. He describes his interactions with Don Francesco di Castro, emphasizing that while England and Spain are generally aligned, their representatives must act as adversaries in this particular conflict. Wotton presents himself as steadfast in reminding Venice of its sovereignty and dignity. The letter concludes with a note that a merchant’s son, traveling through France, will deliver an intercepted packet of letters from the Jesuit Robert Parsons, indicating a covert intelligence effort." ;
    bibo:edition "Pearsall Smith 1907, pp. 462-464" ;
    skos:note "letter_15 in the old chart (Wotton_Jes_Letters)" ;
    opus:authorAsMarked "Henry Wotton" ;
    opus:dateAsMarked "2nd of February, 1606⟨7⟩." ;
    opus:dateIsInferred true ;
    opus:destination [ opus:certainty "inferred" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/place/place_4> ] ;
    opus:destinationAsMarked "London" ;
    opus:incipit "In my audiences here" ;
    opus:originAsMarked "Venice" ;
    opus:recipient [ opus:certainty "certain" ;
            opus:value <https://opus-project.eu/person/person_199> ] ;
    opus:recipientAsMarked "Earl of Salisbury" ;
